<main class="site-content">
    <div class="container">
                    <article id="post-46" class="post-46 page type-page status-publish hentry">
                <header class="entry-header">
                    <h1 class="entry-title">Wilhelm Röntgen</h1>                </header>

                <div class="entry-content">
                    <div class="lang-hint">🇩🇪 <a href="/wilhelm-conrad-rontgen/">Zur deutschen Version</a></div>

<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1600" height="2059" src="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/image.jpg" alt="Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen, Würzburg 1900" class="wp-image-58" srcset="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/image.jpg 1600w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/image-233x300.jpg 233w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/image-796x1024.jpg 796w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/image-768x988.jpg 768w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/image-1194x1536.jpg 1194w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/image-1591x2048.jpg 1591w" sizes="(max-width: 1600px) 100vw, 1600px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen, Würzburg 1900</figcaption></figure>

<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen</h2>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen was born on 27 March 1845 in Remscheid-Lennep, Germany. His parents were the cloth merchant Friedrich Conrad Röntgen and his wife Charlotte, née Frowein. In 1849 the family moved to Apeldoorn in the Netherlands, where he later attended various schools in Apeldoorn and Utrecht.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">From 1865 to 1868 he studied at the Federal Polytechnic School (ETH) in Zurich, receiving his diploma as a mechanical engineer. With his inaugural dissertation <em>Studies on Gases</em>, he obtained a doctorate in philosophy from the University of Zurich in 1868.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">From 1870 to 1872 he was assistant to Professor August Kundt at the Physical Institute in Würzburg. In 1872 he followed Kundt to the newly founded Imperial University of Strasbourg, where he habilitated in 1874. In 1879 he accepted a call as full Professor of Physics at the University of Giessen, where he taught until 1888 and published around 20 scientific papers. In 1888 he was appointed Professor of Physics and Director of the Physical Institute at the Julius Maximilian University of Würzburg – where he would make his most famous discovery.</p>
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity" />
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Life at a Glance</h3>
<div class="roentgen-timeline">
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1845</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Born on 27 March in Remscheid-Lennep, son of a cloth merchant</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1865 – 1868</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Studied mechanical engineering at the Federal Polytechnic School in Zurich (later ETH); PhD in Physics at the University of Zurich</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1870 – 1872</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Assistant to Prof. August Kundt at the Physical Institute, University of Würzburg</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1872 – 1879</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Lecturer and Associate Professor at the Imperial University of Strasbourg</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1879 – 1888</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Full Professor of Physics at the University of Giessen</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1888</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Appointed Professor of Physics and Director of the Physical Institute at the Julius-Maximilians-Universität Würzburg</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item" style="border-left:3px solid #c9a227;padding-left:0.8rem;background:#fffbf0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0">
    <div class="timeline-year" style="color:#b8820a">8 Nov 1895 ★</div>
    <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Discovery of X-rays</strong> in the laboratory of the Physical Institute in Würzburg – one of the most significant discoveries in the history of science</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">23 Jan 1896</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Public lecture at the Physical-Medical Society of Würzburg; anatomist Albert von Kölliker proposes naming the rays &#8222;Röntgen rays&#8220;</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1900</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Appointed Professor at the Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">10 Dec 1901</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">First Nobel Prize in Physics – Röntgen donates the entire prize money to the University of Würzburg</div>
  </div>
  <div class="timeline-item">
    <div class="timeline-year">1923</div>
    <div class="timeline-text">Died on 10 February in Munich, aged 77</div>
  </div>
</div>
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity" />
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow"><h4>The Discovery – 8 November 1895</h4><p>Working late in his laboratory at the Physical Institute in Würzburg, Röntgen observed a previously unknown type of radiation that he called &bdquo;X-rays.&rdquo; It penetrated matter opaque to light – a revolutionary discovery with immediate applications in medicine.</p></blockquote>
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="933" height="500" src="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Roentgenaufnahme-der-Haende.jpg" alt="X-ray of hands – the first ever X-ray images" class="wp-image-25" srcset="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Roentgenaufnahme-der-Haende.jpg 933w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Roentgenaufnahme-der-Haende-300x161.jpg 300w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Roentgenaufnahme-der-Haende-768x412.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 933px) 100vw, 933px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">First X-ray images: Left – the hand of Röntgen&#8217;s wife Bertha, December 1895; Right – anatomist Albert von Kölliker, January 1896</figcaption></figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On 23 January 1896, Röntgen presented his discovery publicly for the first time at the Physical-Medical Society of Würzburg. During the lecture he X-rayed the hand of anatomist Albert von Kölliker, who was so impressed that he proposed the new rays be called <em>Röntgen rays</em> – a name met with enthusiastic applause.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In 1900 he moved to Munich as Professor of Physics at the Ludwig Maximilian University. On 10 December 1901 he received the <strong>first Nobel Prize in Physics</strong>. He donated the entire prize money of 50,000 crowns to the University of Würzburg to support scientific research.</p>
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="590" height="500" src="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Wiener-Presse-5-Januar-1896.jpg" alt="Die Presse (Vienna), 5 January 1896 – first report on the discovery of X-rays" class="wp-image-26" srcset="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Wiener-Presse-5-Januar-1896.jpg 590w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Wiener-Presse-5-Januar-1896-300x254.jpg 300w" sizes="(max-width: 590px) 100vw, 590px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Die Presse (Vienna), 5 January 1896 – first report on the discovery of X-rays</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="640" height="500" src="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Nobelpreis-Urkunde.jpg" alt="Nobel Prize certificate" class="wp-image-23" srcset="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Nobelpreis-Urkunde.jpg 640w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Nobelpreis-Urkunde-300x234.jpg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Nobel Prize certificate for Physics 1901 – first Nobel Prize in history</figcaption></figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Röntgen refused to take out a patent on his discovery, believing it should benefit all of humanity. His scientific legacy comprises nearly 70 published works.</p>
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="500" src="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Student-Roentgen.jpg" alt="Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen as a student" class="wp-image-27" srcset="https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Student-Roentgen.jpg 1024w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Student-Roentgen-300x146.jpg 300w, https://wilhelmconradroentgen.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Student-Roentgen-768x375.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen (centre right) as a student in Zurich, c. 1866</figcaption></figure>                </div>
            </article>
                </div>
</main>

